CONCEPT
        overloading

DESCRIPTION
        This concept is strongly connected with the concept of inheritance.
        A function is called 'overloaded' if it is defined more than once
        in an object. This can happen if the object inherits other objects
        which have defined a function with the same name.

        Usually the overloading is wanted and intended by the inheriting
        object to change the behaviour of the function it overloads.
        To call the overloaded functions from the overloading object the
        ::-operator is used.

        From outside the object only one of the functions can be called
        via call_other() or the like; this will be the topmost of all
        overloaded functions.

        Normally an overloading function is declared the same way as the
        overloaded function, this means it has the same number and types
        of arguments. If an object wants to change the behaviour of the
        function in a way that it can get more arguments than the original
        function, it has to use the modifier 'varargs' or a compiler error
        will be raised.

        If a function by the same name is inherited from multiple sources,
        and not explicitly overloaded, the first inherit will become the
        'dominant' version. Additionally 'nomask' functions will take
        precedence.

        Be aware that the topmost/dominant function replaces *all* other
        functions of the same name in the *whole* object, including all of
        its inherits! Calls without the ::-operator, even in inherited
        files, will always resolve to this dominant function - which may
        lead to unexpected behavior (that may also depend on the order of
        inherits).

        The only exception are functions that have the 'private' modifier.
        For those, objects will still see the local version (but being
        'private' they obviously wont be visible for other objects that
        inherit this code).

EXAMPLES
        File /players/alfe/a.c:

            foo() { write("A"); }
            bar() { foo(); write(" via a "); }

        File /players/alfe/b.c:

            foo() { write("B"); }
            bar() { foo(); write(" via b "); }
            baz() { bar(); }

        File /players/alfe/c.c:

            inherit "players/alfe/a";
            inherit "players/alfe/b";

            foo() {
              a::foo();
              b::foo();
              write("C");
            }

        To call "players/alfe/c"->foo() will now result in the output of
        "ABC".

        A call to "players/alfe/c"->bar() will result in the output
        "ABC via a", as foo() in both "a" and "b" was overloaded by the
        version from "c", and also a::bar() was inherited first and
        therefore became the dominant version within the object.

        The latter can be shown by calling "players/alfe/c"->baz(),
        which will *also* output "ABC via a". Despite baz() being
        defined in "b" it also 'sees' the dominant version a::bar()!
                

        If we change file /players/alfe/a.c to:

            foo() { write("A"); }
            
        file /players/alfe/b.c to:

            private foo() { write("B"); }
            bar() { foo(); write(" via b "); }

        and file /players/alfe/c.c to:

            inherit "players/alfe/a";
            inherit "players/alfe/b";

            foo() {
              a::foo(); // or just ::foo() as there now only is one
                        // visible inherited function foo()
              b::bar(); // b::foo() is no longer visible here
              write("C");
            }
            
        calling "players/alfe/c"->bar() will now result in the output
        "B via b". The 'private' function b::foo() has now become the
        default within "b".

        Consequently a call to "players/alfe/c"->foo() now prints
        "AB via b C".

        Furthermore: without the 'private' modifier this last call
        would result in an endless recursion, as b::bar() would then
        call the topmost function c::foo(), which in turn calls b::bar,
        and so on.

